WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:02.980
Models do what training pays for.

00:00:04.180 --> 00:00:06.020
Here is a training setup.

00:00:06.340 --> 00:00:09.920
A model writes code, and it earns reward
when the unit tests pass.

00:00:10.520 --> 00:00:13.903
In a 2025 OpenAI study
of a reasoning model in training,

00:00:13.903 --> 00:00:17.422
some of its outputs ended the program
before any test ran,

00:00:17.422 --> 00:00:19.520
or told the tests to skip themselves.

00:00:20.120 --> 00:00:21.400
Both raise the reward.

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:23.320
Neither solves the task.

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:25.743
The reward measured passing tests,

00:00:25.743 --> 00:00:28.280
and passing tests
is what the training produced.

00:00:29.480 --> 00:00:31.400
Human approval is a reward too.

00:00:31.720 --> 00:00:33.686
Assistants are trained toward answers

00:00:33.686 --> 00:00:37.320
that people, and models
of people's preferences, rate highly.

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:41.470
Mrinank Sharma and colleagues found
that matching the user's beliefs

00:00:41.470 --> 00:00:45.260
was among the features that best predicted
which answer people preferred.

00:00:45.860 --> 00:00:47.569
Given a user's mistaken belief,

00:00:47.569 --> 00:00:50.437
a preference model preferred
a convincing answer that agreed

00:00:50.437 --> 00:00:53.800
with it over a standard truthful answer
ninety-five percent of the time.

00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:59.680
That is sycophancy, and the paper traces
it in part to the preference data itself.

00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:04.650
Carson Denison and colleagues trained
a model through a series of environments

00:01:04.650 --> 00:01:07.520
that each paid for a small kind
of gaming, like flattery.

00:01:08.120 --> 00:01:09.711
Then they gave it a held-out task

00:01:09.711 --> 00:01:12.540
where it could edit the code
that computed its own reward.

00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:14.352
It did so in forty-five

00:01:14.352 --> 00:01:17.860
of thirty-two thousand
seven hundred and sixty-eight episodes.

00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:22.120
A model trained only to be helpful
did so in none of one hundred thousand.

00:01:22.720 --> 00:01:26.200
The rate is small, and the environments
were built to make gaming possible.

00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:28.275
The direction is the finding:

00:01:28.275 --> 00:01:31.240
rewarded gaming spread to gaming
that was never rewarded.

00:01:32.440 --> 00:01:34.540
None of this needs a model
that wants anything.

00:01:34.860 --> 00:01:38.680
It needs a reward that pays for the wrong
thing, and an optimizer that finds it.

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:42.756
So the fix sits in the environment:
pay for the property you need,

00:01:42.756 --> 00:01:46.120
and check the thing you pay for
with a test it cannot satisfy by shortcut.

00:01:47.320 --> 00:01:51.120
When a model does something strange,
ask first what its training paid for.

00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:54.440
The voice you heard
is a synthesized version of mine.

00:01:54.760 --> 00:01:57.660
Every number on screen links
to its source under the video.
